---
import '../styles/global.css';
---

<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<meta charset="utf-8" />
		<link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
		<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
		<meta name="generator" content={Astro.generator} />
		<title>Architecture — Nalinka Heshan</title>
		<meta
			name="description"
			content="How nalinkaheshan.dev deploys itself: S3, CloudFront and ACM via Terraform, Cloudflare DNS, and GitHub Actions assuming an IAM role over OIDC with no stored AWS keys."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
	<main>
		<header>
			<p><a href="/">Nalinka Heshan</a></p>
			<h1>How this site deploys itself</h1>
		</header>

		<section>
			<p>
				This site is a static Astro build — plain HTML and CSS, no client-side
				framework. Pushing to <code>master</code> on GitHub runs the deploy
				workflow, which builds the site and pushes it live. There's no manual
				step in between.
			</p>
			<p>
				The infrastructure — the S3 bucket, the CloudFront distribution, the
				ACM certificate, and the IAM role GitHub Actions assumes — is defined
				in Terraform, not clicked together by hand in the AWS console.
			</p>
			<p>
				The site sits in a private S3 bucket. It isn't public, and it isn't
				using S3's static website hosting feature. CloudFront reads it through
				Origin Access Control, and the bucket policy only allows reads from
				this one distribution's ARN — not from any CloudFront distribution in
				the account.
			</p>
			<p>
				The TLS certificate is issued by ACM in <code>us-east-1</code>, which is
				where CloudFront requires it to live no matter which region anything
				else runs in.
			</p>
			<p>
				DNS is Cloudflare, not Route 53. The domain is registered through
				Cloudflare Registrar, and its registration agreement requires
				Cloudflare's own nameservers — there's no way to delegate this domain
				to Route 53. The ACM validation record and the apex record pointing at
				CloudFront are both added by hand in Cloudflare, with the proxy
				switched off. With the proxy on, Cloudflare would terminate TLS with
				its own certificate in front of CloudFront, which would make the ACM
				certificate pointless.
			</p>
			<p>
				Deploying doesn't use any stored AWS access keys. GitHub Actions
				authenticates to AWS by requesting a short-lived OIDC token from
				GitHub's own token endpoint and exchanging it for temporary
				credentials scoped to one IAM role. Nothing is stored in the repo or
				in GitHub secrets.
			</p>
			<p>
				The role's trust policy only accepts that token if it's for this exact
				repository and this exact branch. GitHub changed how it writes that
				identity into the token: for repositories created after 15 July 2026,
				the subject claim embeds the numeric owner and repository IDs, not
				just their names —
				<code>repo:nalinka1@35029715/nalinka-heshan@1358027744:ref:refs/heads/master</code>,
				not the plain <code>repo:nalinka1/nalinka-heshan:ref:refs/heads/master</code>
				that most OIDC tutorials still assume. I found this out by decoding the
				token from a live workflow run after role assumption kept failing
				against a trust policy that looked correct.
			</p>
			<p>
				The role itself can only write to this one S3 bucket and invalidate
				this one CloudFront distribution. It can't read anything, and it can't
				touch any other AWS resource.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section aria-labelledby="links-heading">
			<h2 id="links-heading">Links</h2>
			<ul>
				<li><a href="https://github.com/nalinka1/nalinka-heshan/tree/master/terraform">Terraform directory</a></li>
				<li><a href="https://github.com/nalinka1/nalinka-heshan/blob/master/.github/workflows/deploy.yml">Deploy workflow</a></li>
			</ul>
		</section>
	</main>
	</body>
</html>
